.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_GET_CLIENT_RANDOM 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_get_client_random ,
.Nm SSL_get_server_random ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_get_master_key ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_set1_master_key
.Nd get internal TLS/SSL random values and get/set master key
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft size_t
.Fo SSL_get_client_random
.Fa "const SSL *ssl"
.Fa "unsigned char *out"
.Fa "size_t outlen"
.Fc
.Ft size_t
.Fo SSL_get_server_random
.Fa "const SSL *ssl"
.Fa "unsigned char *out"
.Fa "size_t outlen"
.Fc
.Ft size_t
.Fo SSL_SESSION_get_master_key
.Fa "const SSL_SESSION *session"
.Fa "unsigned char *out"
.Fa "size_t outlen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_SESSION_set1_master_key
.Fa "SSL_SESSION *sess"
.Fa "const unsigned char *in"
.Fa "size_t len"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn SSL_get_client_random
extracts the random value sent from the client to the server during the
initial SSL/TLS handshake.
It copies as many bytes as it can of this value into the buffer provided
in
.Fa out ,
which must have at least
.Fa outlen
bytes available.
It returns the total number of bytes that were actually copied.
If
.Fa outlen
is zero,
.Fn SSL_get_client_random
copies nothing, and returns the total size of the client_random value.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_get_server_random
behaves the same, but extracts the random value sent from the server to
the client during the initial SSL/TLS handshake.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_get_master_key
behaves the same, but extracts the master secret used to guarantee the
security of the SSL/TLS session.
This one can be dangerous if misused; see NOTES below.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_set1_master_key
sets the master key value associated with the SSL_SESSION
.Fa sess .
For example, this could be used to set up a session based PSK (see
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_psk_use_session_callback 3 Ns ).
The master key of length
.Fa len
should be provided at
.Fa in .
The supplied master key is copied by the function, so the caller is
responsible for freeing and cleaning any memory associated with
.Fa in .
The caller must ensure that the length of the key is suitable for the
ciphersuite associated with the SSL_SESSION.
.Sh NOTES
You probably shouldn't use these functions.
.Pp
These functions expose internal values from the TLS handshake, for use
in low-level protocols.
You probably should not use them, unless you are implementing something
that needs access to the internal protocol details.
.Pp
Despite the names of
.Fn SSL_get_client_random
and
.Fn SSL_get_server_random ,
they ARE NOT random number generators.
Instead, they return the mostly-random values that were already
generated and used in the TLS protocol.
Using them in place of
.Xr RAND_bytes 3
would be grossly foolish.
.Pp
The security of your TLS session depends on keeping the master key
secret: do not expose it, or any information about it, to anybody.
If you need to calculate another secret value that depends on the master
secret, you should probably use
.Xr SSL_export_keying_material 3
instead, and forget that you ever saw these functions.
.Pp
In current versions of the TLS protocols, the length of client_random
(and also server_random) is always SSL3_RANDOM_SIZE bytes.
Support for other outlen arguments to the
.Xr SSL_get_*_random 3
functions is provided in case of the unlikely event that a future
version or variant of TLS uses some other length there.
.Pp
Finally, though the "client_random" and "server_random" values are
called "random", many TLS implementations will generate four bytes of
those values based on their view of the current time.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_SESSION_set1_master_key
returns 1 on success or 0 on failure.
.Pp
For the other functions, if
.Fa outlen
is greater than 0 then these functions return the number of bytes
actually copied, which will be less than or equal to
.Fa outlen .
If
.Fa outlen
is 0 then these functions return the maximum number of bytes they would
copy -- that is, the length of the underlying field.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssl 7 ,
.Xr RAND_bytes 3 ,
.Xr SSL_export_keying_material 3 ,
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_psk_use_session_callback 3
